nodecode-md-search is a rewrite of kuehnberger's kuehnberger/hermes-qdrant-memory:
https://github.com/kuehnberger/hermes-qdrant-memory
commit 5241e51274c657251776659bfb651aae4ba4bf1d
MIT, Copyright (c) 2026 kuehnberger and the hermes-qdrant-memory contributors (LICENSE)

What was rewritten: the md_search half — the heading-aware markdown chunker
(frontmatter stripped, a section a passage, long sections split on blank
lines), the OR-of-terms ranking over the words of a query, the excerpt
centred on the first matching term, the two corpus rules (an explicit skip
list, and no dot-directory), and the answer's shape: `path  [heading chain]`
and one quoted line a result. In the original that index was SQLite's FTS5,
built by a separate ingest script; here it is an in-memory index the cell
builds from the folders the section names and rebuilds when it is older than
one setting, so there is no database file, no ingest command and no service.
The tokenizer keeps the property the original chose remove_diacritics 2 for: a
query for `grusse` finds `Grüße`.

What was left out, and why: the Qdrant memory provider that the rest of the
plugin is — its six qdrant_* tools, the per-turn recall and capture, and the
on-device fastembed or sentence-transformers embedder — because that is a
separate concern with its own service and its own model download, and a
Nodecode cell already keeps memories in a Qdrant store. The semantic
fallback tier (a second Qdrant collection of embedded passages) went with it,
so this rewrite is lexical only: no embeddings, no vector search, and
therefore no multilingual or paraphrase matching. The `auto`/`always`/`never`
`semantic` parameter went too; `root`, `query` and `limit` are the tool's
arguments as before.
